<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Static preview shown in the Android 12-14 widget picker for the resizable Hijri calendar grid
    widget. Mirrors the live render: a centred one-line month title between the two nav arrows, the
    weekday row, then a full 6-row x 7 month grid with today's cell filled.

    Android 15+ pickers use the generated preview published by HijriWidgetPreviewPublisher, built
    from the same projection as the live widget. This file exists for the two OS generations that
    read android:previewLayout instead, and it is hand-maintained, which is why
    StaticPreviewLayoutTest pins its structure against the live render. A month grid that lost a row,
    dropped its Gregorian sub-digits, or lost its out-of-month dimming looks perfectly plausible in a
    static preview and fails no build.

    The dates are a fixed illustrative snapshot: Muharram 1448, Saturday-first, Urdu, Arabic-Indic
    numerals, with "today" pinned to 19 Muharram / 1 August 2026, a plain Wednesday, so the filled
    highlight is legible without also sitting on a weekend cell. A static preview cannot track the
    real date; what it can do is get the shape right.

    One caveat this layout cannot express: each day cell is a single TextView holding a two-line
    string, so both of its lines share one textStyle. The live render draws them as two Text views at
    *different* weights (the figure Bold, the Gregorian digit Medium), so this preview overstates the
    Gregorian line. That is the hand-maintained-mirror cost noted above; splitting 41 cells into 82
    views is not worth it for a preview only Android 12 to 14 shows. The live weights are asserted in
    GridCellWeightsTest.
-->

